<a name="tdstls"/><h1>TLS/SSL Encryption and Authentication</h1>

<p>SQL Relay supports TLS/SSL encryption and authentication between the TDS client and SQL Relay server.</p>

<blockquote>
<img src="../../images/secure-tls-tds-unsecured.png"/>
</blockquote>
<p>When TLS/SSL encryption and authentication is used:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Communications between the TDS client and SQL Relay server are encrypted.</li>
  <li>TDS clients may optionally validate the server's certificate and identity.</li>
</ul>

<p>When using TLS/SSL encryption and authentication, at minimum, the SQL Relay server must be configured to load a certificate.  For highly secure production environments, this certificate should come from a trusted certificate authority.  In other environments the certificate may be self-signed, or even be borrowed from another server.</p>

<h2>Encryption Only</h2>

<p>The following configuration enables TLS/SSL encryption for an instance of SQL Relay:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"odbc"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"tds"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1433"</font> <font color="#009900">tls</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"yes"</font> <font color="#009900">tlscert</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"/usr/local/firstworks/etc/sqlrserver.pem"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"dsn=mssqlodbc;user=example;password=example;mars=yes"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<ul>
  <li>The <b>tls</b> attribute enables (or disables) TLS/SSL encryption.</li>
  <li>The <b>tlscert</b> attribute specifies the TLS/SSL certificate chain to use.</li>
  <ul>
    <li>(a .pem file is specified in this example, but on Windows systems, a .pfx file or Windows Certificate Store reference must be used. See <a href="../tlscert.html">The tlscert Parameter</a> for details.)</li>
    <li>If the file specified by <b>tlscert</b> doesn't already include the private key, the <b>tlskey</b> attribute may be used to specify a separate file containing it.</li>
  </ul>

</ul>

<p>TDS negotiates encryption in the PRELOGIN exchange, before the login is sent, and the module answers with what the client asked for:</p>

<ul>
  <li>A client that requires encryption gets the whole session encrypted.</li>
  <li>A client that is merely willing to encrypt gets only its login encrypted.  The rest of the session runs in the clear.  This is what a Microsoft SQL Server does by default, and what most TDS clients ask for unless they are configured otherwise.</li>
  <li>A client that says it doesn't support encryption, or that omits the encryption option entirely, isn't pushed into a handshake it never offered.  Its login is refused with a "TLS required" error instead.</li>
</ul>

<p>Which of these a client asks for is a client-side setting.  With freetds, for example, it is the <i>encryption</i> setting in freetds.conf, and a client that validates the server's certificate also needs a <i>ca file</i> there.</p>

<h2>Mutual Authentication</h2>

<p>The <b>tlsvalidate</b> and <b>tlsca</b> attributes described for the <a href="sqlrclientprototls.html">SQLRClient</a>, <a href="mysqlprototls.html">MySQL</a>, and <a href="postgresqlprototls.html">PostgreSQL</a> protocols are honored by the TDS module as well, and enable mutual authentication - the server requests and validates a certificate from the client.  Note, though, that the freetds client cannot present a client certificate, so mutual authentication can only be used with clients that can.</p>

<h2>Performance Considerations</h2>

<p>While TLS/SSL authenticated and encrypted sessions are substantially more secure than standard SQL Relay sessions, several factors contribute to a performance penalty:</p>

<ul>
  <li>When establishing the secure session, a significant amount of data must be sent back and forth between the client and server over multiple network round-trips.</li>
  <li>Some TLS/SSL implementations impose a limit on the amount of data that can be sent or received at once, so more round trips may be required when processing queries.</li>
  <li>Without dedicated encryption hardware and a TLS/SSL implementation that supports it, the computation involved in encrypting and decrypting data can also introduce delays.</li>
</ul>

<p>Any kind of full session encryption should be used with caution in performance-sensitive applications.</p>

